g alt="Emma Roth" data-chromatic="ignore" loading="lazy" width="34" height="34" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" srcset="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=48 1x, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=96 2x" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&amp;w=96"></p><p><span>Emma Roth</span><span><time datetime="2026-11-06T17:27:35+00:00">Nov 6</time></span></p></div><div><p>Will the iPhone 20 ditch the Action Button?</p><p>A <a href="https://weibo.com/5143897135/5362336164743652">Weibo post</a> from leaker Instant Digital suggests Apple could drop the Action button on next year’s iPhone, while merging its functions with the Camera Control button, as reported by <em>MacRumors</em>. Instant Digital claims long-pressing the button will trigger the Action button, while a single press will open the camera.</p></div></div><div id="latest-dmcyOnBvc3Q6MTAyODgxMw=="><p><img alt="The iPhone Duo has more than 100,000 optimized apps, Apple says" data-chromatic="ignore" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-nimg="fill" sizes="100px" srcset="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/268762_iPhone_Duo_AKrales_1188.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=16.666666666667%2C0%2C66.666666666667%2C100&amp;w=32 32w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/268762_iPhone_Duo_AKrales_1188.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=16.666666666667%2C0%2C66.666666666667%2C100&amp;w=48 48w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/268762_iPhone_Duo_AKrales_1188.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all&amp;crop=16.666666666667%2C0%2C66.666666666667%2C100&amp;w=64 64w, 